     1	## Summary
     2	
     3	The sparse vanishing-spike refutation is valid under the literal per-process reading of the conjecture. The failure is quantifier-level: \(\beta\) takes a worst-case supremum over actions, times, and histories, and therefore counts long delays for \(a=0\), even though those delays carry zero payoff and need not create regret.
     4	
     5	The counterexample does not refute a different minimax theorem over a specified class of processes. For that, “possible” must be restated with class-uniform algorithm and regret quantifiers.
     6	
     7	## Concrete Progress
     8	
     9	Verified the solver’s construction:
    10	
    11	Let \(s_n=2^n\), \(U_n\sim \mathrm{Unif}(0,1)\), and
    12	\[
    13	P_t =
    14	\begin{cases}
    15	U_n/2^n, & t=2^n,\\
    16	0, & \text{otherwise.}
    17	\end{cases}
    18	\]
    19	
    20	For every \(h\), choose \(n\) with \(2^n>h\). At \(t=s_n\), action \(a=0\) has
    21	\[
    22	\tau_{s_n}(0)=s_{n+1}-s_n=2^n
    23	\]
    24	almost surely, so
    25	\[
    26	\Pr[h<\tau_{s_n}(0)<\infty\mid P_{1:s_n}]=1.
    27	\]
    28	Thus \(\beta(h)=1\) for all \(h\).
    29	
    30	For any fixed \(a>0\) and horizon \(T\), let
    31	\[
    32	m^*=\max\{m:s_m\le T,\ a<1/s_m\}.
    33	\]
    34	If the set is empty, the comparator reward is zero. Otherwise only \(t<s_{m^*}\) can possibly trade, hence
    35	\[
    36	\mathbb E\sum_{t=1}^T r_t(a)\le a s_{m^*}<1.
    37	\]
    38	Action \(a=0\) earns zero. Therefore the always-zero learner has regret at most \(1\) for every \(T\), despite \(\beta(h)\not\to0\).
    39	
    40	## Claims Or Lemmas
    41	
    42	**Lemma 1: Sparse vanishing spikes refute individual-law necessity.**  
    43	There exists a process with \(\beta(h)=1\) for all \(h\) and a fixed learner with \(R(T)\le 1=o(T)\). This proves the “only if” direction false if interpreted as
    44	\[
    45	\exists \pi\; R^\pi_P(T)=o(T) \implies \beta_P(h)\to0
    46	\]
    47	for each individual process \(P\).
    48	
    49	**Lemma 2: The continuum comparator maximum exists.**  
    50	For finite \(T\), write
    51	\[
    52	M_t=\max_{m:t<s_m\le T} U_m/s_m,
    53	\]
    54	with \(M_t=0\) if the set is empty. Then
    55	\[
    56	\mathbb E r_t(a)=a\,\Pr[M_t>a].
    57	\]
    58	Each \(M_t\) has a continuous distribution on \((0,\infty)\), and the product by \(a\) removes the possible discontinuity at \(0\). The finite sum over \(t\le T\) is continuous on compact \([0,1]\), so the stated \(\max_{a\in[0,1]}\) is attained.
    59	
    60	**Lemma 3: Envelope strengthening.**  
    61	For any eventually positive nondecreasing envelope \(q(T)=o(T)\), one can build a process with \(\beta(h)=1\) for all \(h\) and an always-zero learner satisfying \(R(T)\le q(T)/2\) for all sufficiently large \(T\).
    62	
    63	Take gaps \(s_{n+1}-s_n\to\infty\), for example \(s_n=2^n\), choose \(c_n\in(0,1]\) with \(c_n\le q(s_n)/2\), and set
    64	\[
    65	P_{s_n}=c_nU_n/s_n,\qquad P_t=0\text{ otherwise.}
    66	\]
    67	The same \(a=0\) argument gives \(\beta(h)=1\). If fixed \(a>0\) can trade by horizon \(T\), let \(m^*\) be the last possible crossing spike. Then
    68	\[
    69	\mathbb E\sum_{t=1}^T r_t(a)\le a s_{m^*}<c_{m^*}\le q(s_{m^*})/2\le q(T)/2.
    70	\]
