     1	## Verdict
     2	
     3	SOLVED: yes, as a refutation of the stated “only if” direction under the literal per-process reading.
     4	
     5	## Candidate Solution Or Main Attempt
     6	
     7	The “if” direction in `problem.md` is essentially sound: discretize to \(K\) arms, truncate rewards after \(H\), run a full-information delayed expert algorithm, and pay
     8	\[
     9	O(\sqrt{T H\log K})+\frac{T}{K}+T\beta(H).
    10	\]
    11	Taking \(K=T\) and any \(H(T)\to\infty\) with \(H(T)\log T=o(T)\), for example \(H(T)=\lfloor \sqrt{T/\log(eT)}\rfloor\), gives \(o(T)\) regret when \(\beta(H)\to0\).
    12	
    13	But the converse is false as stated. Define \(s_n=2^n\), let \(U_n\sim \mathrm{Unif}(0,1)\) independently, and set
    14	\[
    15	P_t=
    16	\begin{cases}
    17	U_n/2^n, & t=2^n,\\
    18	0, & \text{otherwise.}
    19	\end{cases}
    20	\]
    21	Then for action \(a=0\), after time \(t=s_n\), the next positive price is at \(s_{n+1}\) almost surely. Hence for every \(h\), choosing \(n\) with \(2^n>h\) gives
    22	\[
    23	\Pr[h<\tau_{s_n}(0)<\infty\mid P_1,\dots,P_{s_n}]=1,
    24	\]
    25	so \(\beta(h)=1\) for all \(h\). Assumption 1 fails maximally.
    26	
    27	Nevertheless, the learner that always posts \(a_t=0\) has regret at most \(1\) for every horizon \(T\). For any fixed \(a>0\), a future spike at \(s_m\) can exceed \(a\) only if \(a<1/s_m\). Let
    28	\[
    29	m^*=\max\{m:s_m\le T,\ a<1/s_m\}.
    30	\]
    31	If no such \(m^*\) exists, the comparator reward is zero. Otherwise only times \(t<s_{m^*}\) can possibly earn reward, so
    32	\[
    33	\mathbb E\sum_{t=1}^T r_t(a)
    34	\le a\,s_{m^*}<1.
    35	\]
    36	For \(a=0\), the reward is zero. Thus
    37	\[
    38	\max_{a\in[0,1]}\mathbb E\sum_{t=1}^T r_t(a)\le 1,
    39	\]
    40	while the always-zero learner earns \(0\). Regret is \(O(1)=o(T)\), despite \(\beta(h)\not\to0\).
    41	
    42	The expected comparator objective is continuous here because it is a finite sum of terms of the form
    43	\[
    44	a\Pr\left[\max_{m:t<s_m\le T} U_m/s_m>a\right],
    45	\]
    46	so the stated `max` exists, not merely a supremum.
    47	
    48	## Concrete Lemmas Or Reductions
    49	
    50	1. Sufficient condition can be weakened to a weighted tail:
    51	\[
    52	\gamma(h):=\sup_t\sup_a\operatorname*{ess\,sup}_{P_{1:t}}
    53	a\,\Pr[h<\tau_t(a)<\infty\mid P_{1:t}].
    54	\]
    55	The truncation proof only needs \(a\Pr[h<\tau_t(a)<\infty]\), not the unweighted probability. Thus \(\gamma(h)\to0\) suffices.
    56	
    57	2. The counterexample has \(\beta(h)=1\) but effectively \(\gamma(h)\to0\), because long-delayed trades only occur at vanishing price scales.
    58	
    59	3. The delayed-learning reduction should be stated for full-information reward vectors revealed after at most \(H\) rounds.
    60	
    61	## Gaps And Failure Points
    62	
    63	The current “only if” target is too strong. Unweighted delay tails can be caused by actions with negligible payoff, especially \(a=0\), and such tails need not create regret.
    64	
    65	A lower bound would need a tail event tied to an action with linear-scale comparator value, not merely a long finite hitting time.
    66	
    67	## Counterexamples Or Obstructions
    68	
    69	The sparse-vanishing-spike construction above is the main obstruction. It shows that uniform conditional tail decay is not necessary for sublinear regret.
    70	
    71	## Literature And Bibliography Candidates
    72	
    73	- Pooria Joulani, András György, Csaba Szepesvári, “Online Learning under Delayed Feedback,” ICML 2013 / PMLR, arXiv:1306.0686. Relevant for black-box delayed online-learning reductions. https://proceedings.mlr.press/v28/joulani13.html
    74	- Kent Quanrud, Daniel Khashabi, “Online Learning with Adversarial Delays,” NeurIPS 2015. Relevant for regret bounds in terms of total delay. https://proceedings.neurips.cc/paper_files/paper/2015/hash/72da7fd6d1302c0a159f6436d01e9eb0-Abstract.html
    75	- Marcelo J. Weinberger, Erik Ordentlich, “On delayed prediction of individual sequences,” IEEE Transactions on Information Theory 48(7):1959–1976, 2002. Foundational fixed-delay prediction reference. https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/iel5/18/21818/01013136.pdf
    76	
    77	## Promising Ideas To Explore
    78	
    79	Replace \(\beta\) by a payoff-weighted or comparator-weighted tail condition. A natural candidate is \(\gamma(h)\to0\), or the horizon-level condition
    80	\[
    81	\sup_a \frac1T\sum_{t=1}^T
    82	\mathbb E\bigl[a\,\mathbf 1\{H(T)<\tau_t(a)<\infty\}\bigr]\to0
    83	\]
    84	for some \(H(T)=o(T/\log T)\).
    85	
    86	For necessity, search for a condition that excludes “vanishing spike” examples and rare irrelevant histories.
    87	
    88	## Notes For Critics
    89	
    90	The counterexample assumes the conjecture is meant per process. If the intended statement is minimax over a class of processes, the quantifiers need to be restated before proving or refuting necessity.
